"Less than half of the noise floor"; "the single loudest thing … almost always" (F7); "chargeable, unrecoverable event" (F6); "Every 'quiet' claim rides on top of these numbers" (F4); "before the concrete cures" (harmless, keep it). The closing hold-room vignette is fine — it is framed as a design intention, not a finding — except the captioning clause (F13).

## Acquittals — well-supported claims the Strategist should defend without blinking

1. The 67-hourly-slot cap and Section 502 exemption awards, including the March 17, 2025 service deadline (CRS, high).
2. The §9.05.8 / $1M / $2-advantage mechanism as a consultant-reported contract term — verified by this reviewer against the underlying DWU page, which the footnote characterizes accurately, current gap $2.36.
3. Peer programs as exception-governance regimes (LCY 2008, HEL 2015, AMS, CPH, SFO 2018, Chennai 2017) — multi-record, correctly hedged, and the "no published operational outcome data" admission is exactly right.
4. DCA CY2025 traffic (24.9M, –5.3%, lowest since 2022) and its three cited drivers, including the seven-week fall 2025 shutdown — four independent high-confidence records.
5. IAD CY2025 records (~29M total, 10.5M international) — high confidence.
6. Concourse E facts (435,000 sq ft, 14 gates, ~46,000 sq ft concessions, 40,000 sq ft United Club, AeroTrain access) — six corroborating records including MWAA primary.
7. The July 29, 2026 announcement's existence, scale range, and *unsigned* status — the draft's footnote 11 hedge matches the ledger exactly.
8. OSHA 29 CFR 1910.95 thresholds; 28 CFR §35.160 "primary consideration"; Part 382's flight-critical information list (as applied to carriers).
9. The 19 July 2024 CrowdStrike outage as the analog-fallback rationale, and the ATL/FLL resilience cases per the curator's ranking.
10. Acoustic ceiling cost ranges ($5–15 new / $18–45 retrofit) presented as analyst calculation with the 1:10:100 heuristic explicitly demoted to direction-only — exactly what the curator required.

